\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Model size and training cost on one GPU. \emph  {Peak memory}: largest allocation during training, with the section resident on the GPU. \emph  {Run time}: one whole fit, for DISCELL including its evaluations every five epochs and the early stop, as the range over the three seeds of the final configuration; for the comparison methods, the training time of the fit compared in this paper, at lineage labels. Every method is fitted on whole sections only. Arrows give the preferred direction; bold marks the best value per section: the fastest finished fit, for DISCELL by its mean. $^{a}$Could not be run on the resources currently available; measured reason: SIMVI, ovarian FFPE: out of GPU memory on a 24 GB card (a further 7.67 GiB requested with 15.87 GiB in use); SIMVI, lung FFPE: out of GPU memory on a 24 GB card (a further 5.16 GiB requested with 21.27 GiB in use); SIMVI, ovarian FF: out of GPU memory on a 24 GB card (a further 40.00 MiB requested with 23.47 GiB in use); MintFlow, ovarian FF: an earlier attempt was stopped for lack of host memory before training (1,157,343 cells, 5,001 genes; an estimated 92 GB needed on a 125 GB host); a retry needs 110 GB free and 102 GB were available.\relax }}{70}{table.caption.88}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:timing}{{17}{70}{Model size and training cost on one GPU. \emph {Peak memory}: largest allocation during training, with the section resident on the GPU. \emph {Run time}: one whole fit, for DISCELL including its evaluations every five epochs and the early stop, as the range over the three seeds of the final configuration; for the comparison methods, the training time of the fit compared in this paper, at lineage labels. Every method is fitted on whole sections only. Arrows give the preferred direction; bold marks the best value per section: the fastest finished fit, for DISCELL by its mean. $^{a}$Could not be run on the resources currently available; measured reason: SIMVI, ovarian FFPE: out of GPU memory on a 24 GB card (a further 7.67 GiB requested with 15.87 GiB in use); SIMVI, lung FFPE: out of GPU memory on a 24 GB card (a further 5.16 GiB requested with 21.27 GiB in use); SIMVI, ovarian FF: out of GPU memory on a 24 GB card (a further 40.00 MiB requested with 23.47 GiB in use); MintFlow, ovarian FF: an earlier attempt was stopped for lack of host memory before training (1,157,343 cells, 5,001 genes; an estimated 92 GB needed on a 125 GB host); a retry needs 110 GB free and 102 GB were available.\relax }{table.caption.88}{}}
\newlabel{tab:timing@cref}{{[table][17][2147483647]17}{[1][44][]70}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Amortisation gap on the simulated sections at the true leak fraction: the distance between an estimate of the posterior mean of $\mathbf  {z}$ and the exact one, in units of the exact posterior standard deviation, averaged over cells, mean of three seeds, by sequencing depth $D$. DISCELL is fitted at the final configuration. The DISCELL encoder is trained without the exact posterior; the other rows are fitted to it directly.\relax }}{70}{table.caption.94}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:planted-gap}{{18}{70}{Amortisation gap on the simulated sections at the true leak fraction: the distance between an estimate of the posterior mean of $\vz $ and the exact one, in units of the exact posterior standard deviation, averaged over cells, mean of three seeds, by sequencing depth $D$. DISCELL is fitted at the final configuration. The DISCELL encoder is trained without the exact posterior; the other rows are fitted to it directly.\relax }{table.caption.94}{}}
\newlabel{tab:planted-gap@cref}{{[table][18][2147483647]18}{[1][45][]70}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces The reads of \cref  {tab:headline} with their intervals, on the four sections and the held-out serial section of the TMA core: mean over three seeds, the range over seeds and, where available, the $200\,\mu \text  {m}$ tile-bootstrap $95\%$ interval (lowest lower and highest upper bound over the seeds). Reconstruction is the held-out log-likelihood in nats per count. Transport{} is the fraction of the noise ceiling (cell-split reliability) recovered by the mean transport{} read, over the panels in the trusted tier ($^{\dagger }$seeds with a trusted panel: TMA core, 2 of 3); its interval is from half-tile subsampling. The same read scored on held-out tiles only is a sensitivity row (\cref  {tab:transport-heldout}). Cycle $R^2$ is read on the top decile of the S and G2M score among held-out cells. $\I  (\text  {niche}; \mathbf  {w})$ is the excess over a within-type permutation floor, in nats. The atlas cosine compares the first programme axis between seeds. The serial section is read through the models fitted on the core (--: not read there). Arrows give the preferred direction; $\approx 0$: expected to be near zero. Unassigned cells are not targets of any read.\relax }}{71}{table.caption.97}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:headline-full}{{19}{71}{The reads of \cref {tab:headline} with their intervals, on the four sections and the held-out serial section of the TMA core: mean over three seeds, the range over seeds and, where available, the $200\um $ tile-bootstrap $95\%$ interval (lowest lower and highest upper bound over the seeds). Reconstruction is the held-out log-likelihood in nats per count. \TermReloc {} is the fraction of the noise ceiling (cell-split reliability) recovered by the mean \termReloc {} read, over the panels in the trusted tier ($^{\dagger }$seeds with a trusted panel: TMA core, 2 of 3); its interval is from half-tile subsampling. The same read scored on held-out tiles only is a sensitivity row (\cref {tab:transport-heldout}). Cycle $R^2$ is read on the top decile of the S and G2M score among held-out cells. $\I (\text {niche}; \vw )$ is the excess over a within-type permutation floor, in nats. The atlas cosine compares the first programme axis between seeds. The serial section is read through the models fitted on the core (--: not read there). Arrows give the preferred direction; $\approx 0$: expected to be near zero. Unassigned cells are not targets of any read.\relax }{table.caption.97}{}}
\newlabel{tab:headline-full@cref}{{[table][19][2147483647]19}{[1][45][]71}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Transport{} scored on held-out tiles only, beside the published read. \emph  {Published}: the read of \cref  {tab:headline}, whose readout is cross-fitted over spatial folds drawn from every tile, so most scored cells lie in the model's training tiles. \emph  {Held-out tiles}: only cells of the model's held-out tiles are scored, and every model quantity is estimated from its training tiles; same niches, panels, ceilings and Unassigned mask. Fraction of the noise ceiling recovered by the mean read, for panels in the trusted tier and for all panels; Read A, the median gap to the target closed, own target; the twin margin, own target. For DISCELL the range over three seeds; Cellina is its neighbour-rewiring counterfactual, fitted on the training tiles and read on the same panels as the first seed. The held-out pool is about three quarters of the published one, so fewer panels reach the trusted tier. Against the published $95\%$ interval, the held-out read moves in both directions: in the trusted tier it lies above on 3, below on 4 and inside on 2 of the 9 DISCELL fits with both reads; over all panels, above on 8, below on 2 and inside on 1 of 11. Read A is lower on held-out tiles on 11 of 12 fits; the twin margin moves by at most 0.024. Arrows give the preferred direction. --: no panel in the tier, or no value; $^{\dagger }$over the seeds with a value.\relax }}{72}{table.caption.107}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:transport-heldout}{{20}{72}{\TermReloc {} scored on held-out tiles only, beside the published read. \emph {Published}: the read of \cref {tab:headline}, whose readout is cross-fitted over spatial folds drawn from every tile, so most scored cells lie in the model's training tiles. \emph {Held-out tiles}: only cells of the model's held-out tiles are scored, and every model quantity is estimated from its training tiles; same niches, panels, ceilings and Unassigned mask. Fraction of the noise ceiling recovered by the mean read, for panels in the trusted tier and for all panels; Read A, the median gap to the target closed, own target; the twin margin, own target. For DISCELL the range over three seeds; Cellina is its neighbour-rewiring counterfactual, fitted on the training tiles and read on the same panels as the first seed. The held-out pool is about three quarters of the published one, so fewer panels reach the trusted tier. Against the published $95\%$ interval, the held-out read moves in both directions: in the trusted tier it lies above on 3, below on 4 and inside on 2 of the 9 DISCELL fits with both reads; over all panels, above on 8, below on 2 and inside on 1 of 11. Read A is lower on held-out tiles on 11 of 12 fits; the twin margin moves by at most 0.024. Arrows give the preferred direction. --: no panel in the tier, or no value; $^{\dagger }$over the seeds with a value.\relax }{table.caption.107}{}}
\newlabel{tab:transport-heldout@cref}{{[table][20][2147483647]20}{[1][48][]72}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Within-type Moran's I of the posterior means, averaged over coordinates weighted by their within-type variance: mean over seeds, with the range. Without the adversary: $\alpha _a= 0$, two seeds; final: the final configuration, three seeds. Target cells only.\relax }}{72}{table.caption.113}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:moran}{{21}{72}{Within-type Moran's I of the posterior means, averaged over coordinates weighted by their within-type variance: mean over seeds, with the range. Without the adversary: $\alphaa = 0$, two seeds; final: the final configuration, three seeds. Target cells only.\relax }{table.caption.113}{}}
\newlabel{tab:moran@cref}{{[table][21][2147483647]21}{[1][49][]72}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces Held-out reconstruction by what the decode may use, in nats per count: mean over three seeds, with the range. \emph  {Full}: log-likelihood per count of the full decode, mean only. The other columns are differences: context over type profile, intrinsic over context (the cell's own $\mathbf  {z}$), full over intrinsic (the cell's own $\mathbf  {w}$). Every difference has a $200\,\mu \text  {m}$ tile-bootstrap $95\%$ interval; all exclude zero except context over type profile on the lung FFPE section, and on the TMA core for one seed. Target cells of held-out tiles; for the serial section, every cell, read through the model fitted to the core.\relax }}{72}{table.caption.114}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:recon-modes}{{22}{72}{Held-out reconstruction by what the decode may use, in nats per count: mean over three seeds, with the range. \emph {Full}: log-likelihood per count of the full decode, mean only. The other columns are differences: context over type profile, intrinsic over context (the cell's own $\vz $), full over intrinsic (the cell's own $\vw $). Every difference has a $200\um $ tile-bootstrap $95\%$ interval; all exclude zero except context over type profile on the lung FFPE section, and on the TMA core for one seed. Target cells of held-out tiles; for the serial section, every cell, read through the model fitted to the core.\relax }{table.caption.114}{}}
\newlabel{tab:recon-modes@cref}{{[table][22][2147483647]22}{[1][50][]72}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces Per-cell divergences and posterior standard deviations over target cells, as the range over three seeds. \emph  {Share}: the fraction of the quantity's total held by the top decile. \emph  {Moran's I}: within-type, on the model's graph (null below $0.006$ everywhere). \emph  {Seed $\rho $}: per-cell Spearman correlation between pairs of seeds.\relax }}{73}{table.caption.117}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:kl-summary}{{23}{73}{Per-cell divergences and posterior standard deviations over target cells, as the range over three seeds. \emph {Share}: the fraction of the quantity's total held by the top decile. \emph {Moran's I}: within-type, on the model's graph (null below $0.006$ everywhere). \emph {Seed $\rho $}: per-cell Spearman correlation between pairs of seeds.\relax }{table.caption.117}{}}
\newlabel{tab:kl-summary@cref}{{[table][23][2147483647]23}{[1][50][]73}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces The leakage{} sweep: every setting refitted at each value of $\kappa $ with three seeds, as the range over seeds; the final configuration is $\kappa = 0.1$ (bold), whose fits are those of \cref  {tab:headline}. Held-out reconstruction in nats per count; NMI of $\mathbf  {z}$ with the type; mirror $R^2$; cycle $R^2$ on the top-decile cycling set; $\I  (\text  {niche}; \mathbf  {w})$, the response's information about the niche as its excess over a within-type permutation floor, in nats. Arrows give the preferred direction; $\approx 0$: expected to be near zero. Unassigned cells are not targets.\relax }}{73}{table.caption.122}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:kappa-sweep}{{24}{73}{The \termSpill {} sweep: every setting refitted at each value of $\kappa $ with three seeds, as the range over seeds; the final configuration is $\kappa = 0.1$ (bold), whose fits are those of \cref {tab:headline}. Held-out reconstruction in nats per count; NMI of $\vz $ with the type; mirror $R^2$; cycle $R^2$ on the top-decile cycling set; $\I (\text {niche}; \vw )$, the response's information about the niche as its excess over a within-type permutation floor, in nats. Arrows give the preferred direction; $\approx 0$: expected to be near zero. Unassigned cells are not targets.\relax }{table.caption.122}{}}
\newlabel{tab:kappa-sweep@cref}{{[table][24][2147483647]24}{[1][51][]73}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Breakdown points $\kappa ^*$ of the contrasts the results claim, per section, on the leakage{} sweep ($\kappa \in \{0, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4\}$, three seeds each). A contrast holds at a grid point if every seed has the sign it has at $\kappa = 0$ and the interval of the mean over seeds (spatial block bootstrap over tiles, two-sided, Bonferroni-adjusted over the $m$ contrasts of the section) excludes $0$. $\kappa ^*$ is the first grid point where it fails; \emph  {above the grid}: it holds at every grid point; \emph  {no finding}: it fails already at $\kappa = 0$; $^{a}$the interval contains $0$, $^{b}$a seed has the opposite sign. Signalling share: the response's share of ligand-receptor (LR) genes against other genes; tumour axis: Kendall's $\tau $ of the response-predicted shift with the true against a false axis. On the serial section only the cycle asymmetry is read, through the core's fits. The tumour axis exists on the ovarian FFPE section only. The marker-pair contrast and transport{} $-$ programme part are trajectories (\cref  {tab:breakdown-traj}). --: not in that section's set.\relax }}{74}{table.caption.123}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:breakdown}{{25}{74}{Breakdown points $\kappa ^*$ of the contrasts the results claim, per section, on the \termSpill {} sweep ($\kappa \in \{0, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4\}$, three seeds each). A contrast holds at a grid point if every seed has the sign it has at $\kappa = 0$ and the interval of the mean over seeds (spatial block bootstrap over tiles, two-sided, Bonferroni-adjusted over the $m$ contrasts of the section) excludes $0$. $\kappa ^*$ is the first grid point where it fails; \emph {above the grid}: it holds at every grid point; \emph {no finding}: it fails already at $\kappa = 0$; $^{a}$the interval contains $0$, $^{b}$a seed has the opposite sign. Signalling share: the response's share of ligand-receptor (LR) genes against other genes; tumour axis: Kendall's $\tau $ of the response-predicted shift with the true against a false axis. On the serial section only the cycle asymmetry is read, through the core's fits. The tumour axis exists on the ovarian FFPE section only. The marker-pair contrast and \termReloc {} $-$ programme part are trajectories (\cref {tab:breakdown-traj}). --: not in that section's set.\relax }{table.caption.123}{}}
\newlabel{tab:breakdown@cref}{{[table][25][2147483647]25}{[1][51][]74}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {27}{\ignorespaces Sensitivity of the final configuration to the assumptions of the model, each arm refitted with one setting changed. For the final configuration, the mean over its seeds with the range; for an arm, its mean and, in brackets, its move from the final configuration in units of the final configuration's standard deviation over seeds; \emph  {Seeds} gives the number of seeds fitted. Reconstruction is held-out, in nats per count; $\I  (\text  {niche}; \mathbf  {w})$ is the excess over the within-type floor, in nats. \emph  {MLP}: the residual neighbour composition in the intrinsic latent, as the share of the block's within-type variance that the nonlinear probe explains beyond the permutation floor, $1 - e^{-2\,\mathrm  {excess}}$ (as in \cref  {tab:probe}), in \%. \emph  {Transport}: the fraction of the noise ceiling recovered by the mean transport{} read, trusted tier (as in \cref  {tab:headline}); $^{\dagger }$over the seeds with a trusted panel. The leak fraction{} is set per cell from its neighbours' depth or density relative to its own, or per gene, at the same mean value; the false-positive floor is fixed once per section, or per cell scaled by its area. Arrows give the preferred direction, so a move in the direction of a column's arrow is an improvement. Unassigned cells are not targets.\relax }}{75}{table.caption.130}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sensitivity}{{27}{75}{Sensitivity of the final configuration to the assumptions of the model, each arm refitted with one setting changed. For the final configuration, the mean over its seeds with the range; for an arm, its mean and, in brackets, its move from the final configuration in units of the final configuration's standard deviation over seeds; \emph {Seeds} gives the number of seeds fitted. Reconstruction is held-out, in nats per count; $\I (\text {niche}; \vw )$ is the excess over the within-type floor, in nats. \emph {MLP}: the residual neighbour composition in the intrinsic latent, as the share of the block's within-type variance that the nonlinear probe explains beyond the permutation floor, $1 - e^{-2\,\mathrm {excess}}$ (as in \cref {tab:probe}), in \%. \emph {\TermReloc }: the fraction of the noise ceiling recovered by the mean \termReloc {} read, trusted tier (as in \cref {tab:headline}); $^{\dagger }$over the seeds with a trusted panel. The \termSpillFrac {} is set per cell from its neighbours' depth or density relative to its own, or per gene, at the same mean value; the false-positive floor is fixed once per section, or per cell scaled by its area. Arrows give the preferred direction, so a move in the direction of a column's arrow is an improvement. Unassigned cells are not targets.\relax }{table.caption.130}{}}
\newlabel{tab:sensitivity@cref}{{[table][27][2147483647]27}{[1][57][]75}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {28}{\ignorespaces The counterfactual claim head to head: DISCELL's transport{} prediction against Cellina's neighbour-rewiring counterfactual, scored with the same transport{} reads (published read, as in \cref  {tab:headline}). Cellina is refitted on the training tiles only, decoded at its posterior mean, and read on the panels, niches, ceilings and bootstrap draws of DISCELL's first seed, which is shown beside it with the $95\%$ intervals (half-tile subsampling for the fraction, tile bootstrap for Read A; to two decimals); DISCELL's range over three seeds is in \cref  {tab:transport-heldout}. \emph  {Panels}: panels in the trusted tier. Read A: the median gap to the target closed; twin margin: how much closer a cell's prediction is to its own target than to a random cell's. \emph  {Parts of the prediction}: DISCELL's prediction from the response programmes alone and from the leakage{} alone (the parts are not additive). $^{a}$Not mapped: Cellina has no leakage{} channel, so its counterfactual cannot be split into these parts. Arrows give the preferred direction (the parts have none); bold marks the best value per section, of the two estimates. Unassigned cells are not targets.\relax }}{76}{table.caption.131}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cellina-cf}{{28}{76}{The counterfactual claim head to head: DISCELL's \termReloc {} prediction against Cellina's neighbour-rewiring counterfactual, scored with the same \termReloc {} reads (published read, as in \cref {tab:headline}). Cellina is refitted on the training tiles only, decoded at its posterior mean, and read on the panels, niches, ceilings and bootstrap draws of DISCELL's first seed, which is shown beside it with the $95\%$ intervals (half-tile subsampling for the fraction, tile bootstrap for Read A; to two decimals); DISCELL's range over three seeds is in \cref {tab:transport-heldout}. \emph {Panels}: panels in the trusted tier. Read A: the median gap to the target closed; twin margin: how much closer a cell's prediction is to its own target than to a random cell's. \emph {Parts of the prediction}: DISCELL's prediction from the response programmes alone and from the \termSpill {} alone (the parts are not additive). $^{a}$Not mapped: Cellina has no \termSpill {} channel, so its counterfactual cannot be split into these parts. Arrows give the preferred direction (the parts have none); bold marks the best value per section, of the two estimates. Unassigned cells are not targets.\relax }{table.caption.131}{}}
\newlabel{tab:cellina-cf@cref}{{[table][28][2147483647]28}{[1][57][]76}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces Recovery on simulated sections at the final configuration, scaled to the simulated world. Three worlds per planted leak fraction{}, each fitted with three model seeds; the range over the nine fits (\emph  {Fits}). NMI of the intrinsic latent's posterior mean with the planted type; \emph  {CCA}: the first canonical correlation of the response's posterior mean with the planted response; \emph  {Loadings}: the principal-angle cosine of the fitted against the planted loadings; \emph  {Comp.\ $R^2$}: the within-type $R^2$ of neighbour composition from the intrinsic latent. \emph  {Misspecified $\kappa $}: the worlds planted at $\kappa = 0.2$, fitted at each assumed rate (the matched rate in bold). \emph  {References}: NMI of clusters of the counts and of the planted intrinsic state, the composition $R^2$ of the planted state, and the loading cosine of a random subspace (its mean and 95th percentile), each as the range over the three worlds and both planted rates. A fit is stopped early on $15\%$ of held-out tiles; every read covers all cells. Arrows give the preferred direction.\relax }}{76}{table.caption.138}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:synthetic}{{29}{76}{Recovery on simulated sections at the final configuration, scaled to the simulated world. Three worlds per planted \termSpillFrac {}, each fitted with three model seeds; the range over the nine fits (\emph {Fits}). NMI of the intrinsic latent's posterior mean with the planted type; \emph {CCA}: the first canonical correlation of the response's posterior mean with the planted response; \emph {Loadings}: the principal-angle cosine of the fitted against the planted loadings; \emph {Comp.\ $R^2$}: the within-type $R^2$ of neighbour composition from the intrinsic latent. \emph {Misspecified $\kappa $}: the worlds planted at $\kappa = 0.2$, fitted at each assumed rate (the matched rate in bold). \emph {References}: NMI of clusters of the counts and of the planted intrinsic state, the composition $R^2$ of the planted state, and the loading cosine of a random subspace (its mean and 95th percentile), each as the range over the three worlds and both planted rates. A fit is stopped early on $15\%$ of held-out tiles; every read covers all cells. Arrows give the preferred direction.\relax }{table.caption.138}{}}
\newlabel{tab:synthetic@cref}{{[table][29][2147483647]29}{[1][60][]76}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces A planted per-cell response on simulated sections (planted $\kappa = 0.2$, final configuration unchanged). In $10\%$ of the cells of one type, spread across niches, the log-rate is shifted along the planted response programme by $s$ times the size of the planted niche effect (\emph  {Shift}, its norm in log-rate units); $s = 0$ is the null. \emph  {KL AUC}: the per-cell divergence of the response posterior from its prior separating the planted cells from the other cells of their type; \emph  {$\mathbf  {w}$ corr.}: correlation of the response's deviation from its prior mean, along the planted direction, with the plant; \emph  {$\mathbf  {z}$ absorption}: the same for the intrinsic latent, read through the decoder along the planted direction; \emph  {Recon. gain}: held-out gain in nats per count of the full decode over the response at its prior mean, on planted cells; \emph  {Spearman} and \emph  {top-decile overlap}: agreement of the per-cell divergence between seeds. Ranges over three model seeds (seed pairs for the last two). The rule, fixed in advance and applied to world 0: the channel detects at $s$ if every seed has KL AUC $\geq 0.8$ and every seed pair a Spearman correlation $\geq 0.5$; worlds 1 and 2 replicate. No strength is detected. The planted subset cannot be predicted from neighbour composition or from composition and the image descriptor (five-fold cross-validated AUC 0.44--0.54 within the planted type, logistic and forest classifiers, all three worlds). The arrow gives the preferred direction.\relax }}{77}{table.caption.144}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:planted-percell}{{30}{77}{A planted per-cell response on simulated sections (planted $\kappa = 0.2$, final configuration unchanged). In $10\%$ of the cells of one type, spread across niches, the log-rate is shifted along the planted response programme by $s$ times the size of the planted niche effect (\emph {Shift}, its norm in log-rate units); $s = 0$ is the null. \emph {KL AUC}: the per-cell divergence of the response posterior from its prior separating the planted cells from the other cells of their type; \emph {$\vw $ corr.}: correlation of the response's deviation from its prior mean, along the planted direction, with the plant; \emph {$\vz $ absorption}: the same for the intrinsic latent, read through the decoder along the planted direction; \emph {Recon. gain}: held-out gain in nats per count of the full decode over the response at its prior mean, on planted cells; \emph {Spearman} and \emph {top-decile overlap}: agreement of the per-cell divergence between seeds. Ranges over three model seeds (seed pairs for the last two). The rule, fixed in advance and applied to world 0: the channel detects at $s$ if every seed has KL AUC $\geq 0.8$ and every seed pair a Spearman correlation $\geq 0.5$; worlds 1 and 2 replicate. No strength is detected. The planted subset cannot be predicted from neighbour composition or from composition and the image descriptor (five-fold cross-validated AUC 0.44--0.54 within the planted type, logistic and forest classifiers, all three worlds). The arrow gives the preferred direction.\relax }{table.caption.144}{}}
\newlabel{tab:planted-percell@cref}{{[table][30][2147483647]30}{[1][61][]77}}
